L000407	{\it \noindent \textbf{Some applications --}} We now discuss some important consequences of Theorem~\ref{theo1}, starting with two corollaries that are particularly relevant for kinetic theory.
L000408	
L000409	\begin{corollary}\label{cor1}
L000410	If the non-hydrodynamic spectrum possesses a fastest relaxation timescale at $k=0$ in one inertial frame, then the same is true in all inertial frames.
L000411	\end{corollary}
L000412	
L000413	\begin{corollary}\label{cor2}
L000414	If the non-hydrodynamic spectrum is gapped at $k=0$ in one inertial frame, then the same is true in all inertial frames.
L000415	\end{corollary}
L000416	
L000417	Both results follow directly from Theorem~\ref{theo1}, by choosing $a=0$ and finite $b$ in the first case, and finite $a$ with $b=\infty$ in the second.
L000418	
L000419	Corollary~\ref{cor2} has an important implication. In \cite{GavassinoConvergence:2024xwf} it was shown that, in kinetic theory, a gap in the non-hydrodynamic spectrum guarantees that all hydrodynamic dispersion relations admit expansions as power series in $k$ with a finite radius of convergence.
L000420	The proof does not rely on the background being at rest, and therefore the result persists under boosts. Combining this with Corollary~\ref{cor2}, we obtain the following statement:
L000421	
L000422	\begin{corollary}
L000423	If the non-hydrodynamic spectrum of a kinetic theory is gapped at $k=0$ in one inertial frame, then the hydrodynamic dispersion relations possess non-vanishing convergence radii in all inertial frames.
L000424	\end{corollary}
L000425	
L000426	This means that, if a genuine separation of scales exists, density-frame hydrodynamics \cite{ArmasDensityFrame2020mpr,Basar:2024qxd,BhambureDensityFrame2024axa} is well defined up to infinite order in derivatives in every inertial frame\footnote{Density-frame hydrodynamics posits that, in any inertial frame (not necessarily comoving), the conserved fluxes $J_I^k$ admit an expansion in powers of \emph{spatial} derivatives of the conserved densities $J_I^0$. Equivalently, that the hydrodynamic eigenfrequencies $\omega$ can be expanded in powers of the spatial wavevector $k^j$.}.
L000427	
L000428	{\it \noindent \textbf{Convergence radius of boosted hydrodynamics --}} Consider a kinetic description of a system admitting a single conserved current, $\partial_\mu J^\mu = 0$.
L000429	Assume that its nonhydrodynamic spectrum is gapped in the rest frame, with slowest
L000430	relaxation rate $a$. In a frame boosted with velocity $v$, the corresponding spectral gap
L000431	is $a' \geq a(1{-}v)/\gamma$. In that boosted frame, the unique hydrodynamic mode $\omega'(k')$ propagating along the
L000432	$x$--direction solves
L000433	\begin{equation}
L000434	(\s + \E^1 ik')\,\Psi = i\omega'\Psi \, ,
L000435	\end{equation}
L000436	where we work on a basis for which $\E^0 = 1$. Upon complexifying $k'$, this equation
L000437	defines an eigenvalue problem for the perturbed family $\s(ik') \equiv \s + ik'\E^1$.
L000438	Since both $\s$ and $\E^1$ are Hermitian (with $||\E^1||\leq 1$), and $0$ is a non-degenerate eigenvalue of $\s$, standard results from analytic perturbation
L000439	theory apply \cite[\S II.3.5]{Kato_Perturbation_Theory}. In particular,
L000440	there exists a unique, analytic hydrodynamic mode $\{ \omega'(k'), \Psi(k') \}$
L000441	in a neighborhood of $k'=0$, whose radius of convergence $R'$ satisfies the lower bound \cite{Kato1949_Perturbation_I}
L000442	\begin{equation}
L000443	\boxed{R' \ge \frac{a'}{2\,||{\E^1}||} \ge \frac{a(1-v)}{2\gamma} \, .}
L000444	\end{equation}
L000445	
L000446	
L000447	
L000448	
L000449	\newpage
L000450	{\it \noindent \textbf{About time dilation --}} It is natural to expect that, as the velocity of the fluid approaches the speed of light, all nonhydrodynamic modes should effectively ``freeze'' due to time dilation. From this viewpoint, one would predict that the upper bound on $i\omega'$ should vanish as $v\!\to\!1$. Figure \ref{fig:splitting} shows that the opposite happens: the upper bound diverges. Where does our intuition fail?
L000451	
L000452	The time-dilation argument implicitly assumes that signals are advected with the fluid element. Indeed, if, in the rest frame, a signal propagates from $(t,x)=(0,0)$ to $(t,x)=(\Delta t,0)$ before decaying, then in a frame where the medium moves at velocity $v$, the signal travels from $(t',x')=(0,0)$ to $(t',x')=(\gamma \Delta t,\gamma v \Delta t)$. Under this assumption, one finds that $i\omega'\sim 1/(\gamma\Delta t)$, which tends to zero as $v\to 1$, in line with expectations.
L000453	The problem appears if the signal has a nonzero rest-frame propagation speed $w$. If in the rest frame it moves from $(t,x)=(0,0)$ to $(t,x)=(\Delta t,- w \Delta t)$, then in the boosted frame it travels to
L000454	$(t',x')=(\gamma \Delta t- \gamma v w \Delta t,\, - \gamma w \Delta t+\gamma v \Delta t)$, which leads to $i\omega' \sim 1/[\gamma(1-vw)\Delta t]$.
L000455	Differentiating with respect to $v$ then shows that, for $v<w$, the boosted frequency \emph{increases} with $v$, in sharp contrast with the simple time-dilation picture. Only once $v>w$ does time dilation dominate, forcing the frequency downward. In the limiting case of a luminal signal ($w=1$), we recover $i\omega' \sim 1/[\gamma(1-v)\Delta t]$, which has the same $v$--dependence as the upper bound in \eqref{theboundoni}, and diverges as $v\rightarrow 1$
L000456	
L000457	The argument above suggests that, if the medium supports signal propagation
L000458	only inside an ``acoustic cone'' narrower than the light cone, then
L000459	the boosted nonhydrodynamic spectrum should obey bounds sharper than
L000460	\eqref{theboundoni}. This is made precise in the theorem below.
L000461	\vspace{-0.1cm}
L000462	\begin{theorem}\label{theo2}
L000463	Consider a medium whose $k{=}0$ nonhydrodynamic spectrum lies within some interval $a \le i\omega \le b$ (with $a\ge 0$) in the rest frame. Assume further that signal propagation in the rest frame is bounded by a maximal speed $w\,{\le}\, 1$. Then, for an observer moving at speed $v\,{\geq}\,0$ relative to the medium, the corresponding $k'{=}0$ nonhydrodynamic frequencies are confined within the interval
L000464	\vspace{-0.3cm}
L000465	\begin{equation}\label{theboundoni2}
L000466	\boxed{\frac{a(1{-}vw)}{\gamma} \leq i\omega' \leq \frac{b}{\gamma(1{-}vw)}\, .} \end{equation}
L000467	\end{theorem}
L000468	\begin{proof}
L000469	The characteristic propagation speeds $v_n$ of signals traveling along the
L000470	$x$--direction are determined by the condition
L000471	$\det(\E^1 - v_n \E^0)=0$ \cite{Hishcock1983}. In a basis where $\E^0 = 1$,
L000472	the $v_n$ coincide with the eigenvalues of $\E^1$, so imposing that no signal
L000473	exceed the maximal speed $w$ is equivalent to the operator bound
L000474	$||\E^1|| \le w$. With this identification, the derivation of
L000475	Theorem~\ref{theo1} carries over unchanged when $\mathcal{O}$ is chosen to
L000476	comove with the medium. The only modification is that, in
L000477	Eq.~\eqref{Nonhydromodes1}, the denominator is now bounded between
L000478	$1 - v w$ and $1 + v w$. Likewise, in the final step of
L000479	\eqref{patatuzza}, we can replace $1 - v^2$ with $1 - v^2 w^2$.
L000480	\end{proof}
L000481	In figure \ref{fig:tighter}, we plot the bounds \eqref{theboundoni2} with $a=b=1$, for various values of $w$. In the Supplementary Material, we test such bounds with some randomly-generated models.
L000482	
L000483	\begin{figure}[h!]
