L000618	\section*{Proof of Theorem 1 within kinetic theory}
L000619	
L000620	
L000621	\subsection{Mathematical set up}
L000622	
L000623	We focus on a non-degenerate gas of spinless particles, and write the kinetic distribution function as $f=f_{\text{eq}}(1+\psi)$, where $f_{\text{eq}}(p^j)$ is a uniform equilibrium state, and $\psi(x^\mu,p^j)$ is a linear perturbation. Working in the rest frame of $\mathcal{O}$ (who need not comove with $f_{\text{eq}}$), and assuming $\psi\propto e^{i\omega t-ik_j x^j}$, the linearized Boltzmann equation reads
L000624	\begin{equation}\label{Boltzmann}
L000625	\left(i\omega-w^j ik_j\right)\psi =I \psi \, ,
L000626	\end{equation}
L000627	where $I$ is the (Friedrichs extension \cite[\S 2.3]{TeschlBook} of the) linearized collision operator\footnote{In some references, one writes $I=-\frac{1}{p^0}L$ \cite{RochaBranchcut:2024cge,GavassinoGapless:2024rck}.}, and $w^j=p^j/p^0$. Working within the Hilbert space $\mathcal{H}=L^2(\mathbb{R}^3,f_{\text{eq}}d^3 p)$, with inner product
L000628	\begin{equation}
L000629	(\phi,\psi)=\int \dfrac{d^3 p}{(2\pi)^3} f_{\text{eq}} \phi^* \psi  \, ,
L000630	\end{equation}
L000631	and norm $||\psi||=\sqrt{(\psi,\psi)}$, and assuming a non-vanishing cross section, one finds the following properties \cite{GavassinoConvergence:2024xwf}:
L000632	\begin{equation}
L000633	\begin{split}
L000634	&I\psi=0 \text{ if and only if }\psi\in \text{span}(1,p^\nu) \, , \\
L000635	&(\phi,I\psi)^*=(\psi, I\phi) \, , \\
L000636	&(\psi,I\psi)\geq 0 \, , \\
L000637	&(\phi,w^j\psi)^*=(\psi,w^j\phi) \, , \\
L000638	&(\psi,w^j \psi)\in [-||\psi||^2,||\psi||^2] \, . \\
L000639	\end{split}
L000640	\end{equation}
L000641	Thus, the structure mirrors that of the main text, under the replacements $\Psi \rightarrow \psi$, $\E^0\rightarrow 1$, $\E^j \rightarrow w^j$, $\s \rightarrow I$, $\Phi^\dagger \Psi \rightarrow (\phi,\psi)$, and $\{\Phi_I\}\rightarrow \{1,p^0,p^1,p^2,p^3\}$. Indeed, if we decompose $\psi$ into an orthonormal basis, and denote the list of linear combination coefficients by $\Psi$, we recover precisely the formalism of the main text, with $D=\infty$.
L000642	
L000643	There is, however, one caveat. In an infinite-dimensional Hilbert space, the excitation spectrum generally includes both point and continuous components, and equation \eqref{Boltzmann} only captures the former. To characterize the full spectrum, we adopt the following broader definition \cite[Lecture 18, Th. 1]{Bhatiabook}:
L000644	\begin{definition}\label{def1}
L000645	A wavevector $k^\mu =(\omega,k^j)$ is an eigenmode of the theory if there exists a sequence $\{\psi_n\}_{n=1}^{\infty}$ of normalized states (i.e. $||\psi_n||=1$) such that
L000646	\begin{equation}\label{limitone}
L000647	||\left(I-i\omega+w^j ik_j\right)\psi_n|| \rightarrow 0 \, .
L000648	\end{equation}
L000649	\end{definition}
L000650	
L000651	\subsection{Conservation laws and non-hydrodynamic modes}
L000652	
L000653	In linearized kinetic theory, the perturbations of the conserved currents associated with a state $\psi$ take the form
L000654	\begin{equation}
L000655	\delta J^\mu = \int \dfrac{d^3 p}{(2\pi)^3} f_{\text{eq}} \dfrac{p^\mu}{p^0} \phi =
L000656	\begin{bmatrix}
L000657	(1,\psi) \\
L000658	(w^1,\psi) \\
L000659	(w^2,\psi) \\
L000660	(w^3,\psi) \\
L000661	\end{bmatrix} \, ,
L000662	\qquad
L000663	\delta T^{\mu\nu} = \int \dfrac{d^3 p}{(2\pi)^3} f_{\text{eq}} \dfrac{p^\mu}{p^0} p^\nu \phi =
L000664	\begin{bmatrix}
L000665	(p^\nu,\psi) \\
L000666	(w^1 p^\nu,\psi) \\
L000667	(w^2 p^\nu,\psi) \\
L000668	(w^3 p^\nu,\psi) \\
L000669	\end{bmatrix} \, ,
L000670	\end{equation}
L000671	which represent the particle-number current and the stress-energy tensor. For a generic collision cross section, these exhaust the set of conserved currents \cite[\S 2.4]{cercignani_book}. In line with the definition used in the main text, we therefore call an eigenmode $k'^\mu=\omega' u'^\mu$ non-hydrodynamic relative to an observer $\mathcal{O}'$ (with four-velocity $u'^\mu$) if the associated state $\psi$ (or the sequence of states $\psi_n$ of Definition~\ref{def1}) carries no conserved density, i.e.
L000672	\begin{equation}
L000673	u'_\mu \delta J^\mu =u'_\mu \delta T^{\mu \nu}=0 \, .
L000674	\end{equation}
L000675	
L000676	There is, however, a key distinction from the finite-dimensional setting. In that case, $\omega'\neq 0$ is required, since $\omega'=0$ would imply $\Psi\in\ker(\s)$, meaning that the state is a genuine equilibrium. In the infinite-dimensional kinetic-theory case, it may happen that none of the $\psi_n$ lies in $\ker(I)$, while the limit \eqref{limitone} holds with $k^\mu=0$. In that case, $\omega'=0$ belongs to the continuous non-hydrodynamic spectrum, which is then said to be gapless \cite{GavassinoGapless:2024rck}. For our purposes, this subtlety is immaterial, since the theorem already allows the lower endpoint of the interval $a\leq i\omega \leq b$ to satisfy $a=0$.
L000677	
L000678	
L000679	
L000680	\subsection{Bounds on the collision operator}
L000681	\vspace{-0.2cm}
L000682	
L000683	From the definitions above, a frequency $\omega$ lies in the $k=0$ non-hydrodynamic spectrum relative to $\mathcal{O}$ if there exists a sequence of states $\psi_n$ satisfying
L000684	\begin{equation}
L000685	||\psi_n||=1 \, , \quad \qquad (1,\psi_n)=(p^\nu,\psi_n)=0 \, , \quad \qquad ||\left(I-i\omega\right)\psi_n|| \rightarrow 0 \, .
L000686	\end{equation}
L000687	Equivalently, $i\omega$ belongs to the spectrum of $I$ restricted to the Hilbert subspace $\{1,p^\nu\}^{\perp}$. Using \cite[\S 2.4, Th.~2.19]{TeschlBook} together with the assumed bound $a\leq i\omega\leq b$, we obtain the variational inequalities
L000688	\begin{equation}\label{bounduzzo}
L000689	\begin{split}
L000690	& \inf_{\{1,p^\nu\}^{\perp}} \dfrac{(\psi,I\psi)}{(\psi,\psi)} = \inf_{\{1,p^\nu\}^{\perp}} \text{Spectrum}(I)\geq a \, , \\
L000691	& \sup_{\{1,p^\nu\}^{\perp}} \dfrac{(\psi,I\psi)}{(\psi,\psi)} = \sup_{\{1,p^\nu\}^{\perp}} \text{Spectrum}(I)\leq b \, ,\\
L000692	\end{split}
L000693	\end{equation}
L000694	which directly parallel the bounds on $\Psi^\dagger \s\Psi/(\Psi^\dagger \Psi)$ established in the main text.
L000695	
L000696	\vspace{-0.2cm}
L000697	\subsection{Bounds on boosted spectra}
L000698	\vspace{-0.2cm}
L000699	
L000700	We are now ready to derive bounds on the $k'=0$ non-hydrodynamic spectrum relative to an observer $\mathcal{O}'$ that moves with speed $v$ relative to $\mathcal{O}$. The algebra is essentially identical to that of the main text, modulo some technicalities.
L000701	
L000702	As usual, we orient the axes such that the fourvelocity of $\mathcal{O}'$ is $u'^\mu=(\gamma,-\gamma v,0,0)$. Then, $\omega'$ is a non-hydrodynamic frequency provided that there exists a sequence of states $\psi_n$ with the following properties:
L000703	\begin{equation}\label{gringooogrigoo}
L000704	||\psi_n||=1 \, , \quad \qquad (1{+}vw^1,\psi_n)=([1{+}vw^1] p^\nu,\psi_n)=0 \, , \quad \qquad ||\left[I-i\gamma\omega'(1{+}vw^1)\right]\psi_n|| \rightarrow 0 \, .
L000705	\end{equation}
L000706	Using the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality, we have that
L000707	\begin{equation}
L000708	|(\psi_n,\left[I-i\gamma\omega'(1{+}vw^1)\right]\psi_n)|\leq ||\left[I-i\gamma\omega'(1{+}vw^1)\right]\psi_n|| \rightarrow 0 \, ,
L000709	\end{equation}
L000710	which implies
L000711	\begin{equation}
L000712	[1-v(\psi_n,w^1\psi_n)]\bigg[\dfrac{(\psi_n,I\psi_n)}{1-v(\psi_n,w^1\psi_n)}-i\gamma \omega'\bigg]\rightarrow 0\, .
L000713	\end{equation}
L000714	But since $|(\psi_n,w^1\psi_n)|\leq ||\psi_n||^2=1$, the first square bracket is larger $1-v$, and thus cannot tend zero. Hence, the second square bracket must approach zero, giving
L000715	\begin{equation}
L000716	\dfrac{(\psi_n,I\psi_n)}{1-v(\psi_n,w^1\psi_n)}\rightarrow i\gamma \omega'   \, .
L000717	\end{equation}
L000718	Thus, if we can prove that each number in this sequence is bounded between $a(1{-}v)$ and $b/(1{-}v)$, we are done.
L000719	
L000720	
L000721	Let $\{\phi_I\}_{I=0}^4$ be an orthonormal basis of $\text{span}(1,p^\nu)$, and define $\Bar{\psi}_n=\psi_n-\sum_I (\phi_I,\psi_n)\phi_I$. Then, we have that
L000722	\begin{equation}
L000723	\Bar{\psi}_n \in \{1,p^\nu\}^{\perp} \, , \quad\qquad (\Bar{\psi}_n,I\Bar{\psi}_n)=(\psi_n,I\psi_n) \, , \quad\qquad (\Bar{\psi}_n,\Bar{\psi}_n)=1-\sum\nolimits_I |(\phi_I,\psi_n)|^2 \, ,
L000724	\end{equation}
L000725	and the upper bound is immediately proven:
L000726	\begin{equation}
L000727	\dfrac{(\psi_n,I\psi_n)}{1-v(\psi_n,w^1\psi_n)} =\dfrac{(\Bar{\psi}_n,I\Bar{\psi}_n)}{1-v(\psi_n,w^1\psi_n)} \leq \dfrac{b (\Bar{\psi}_n,\Bar{\psi}_n)}{1-v} \leq \dfrac{b}{1-v}\, .
L000728	\end{equation}
L000729	To obtain the lower bound, we also need the second condition of \eqref{gringooogrigoo}, namely $(\phi_I,\psi_n)=-v(\phi_I,w^1\psi_n)$, so that
L000730	\begin{equation}
L000731	\begin{split}
L000732	&\dfrac{(\psi_n,I\psi_n)}{1-v(\psi_n,w^1\psi_n)}= \dfrac{(\Bar{\psi}_n,I\Bar{\psi}_n)}{1-v(\psi_n,w^1\psi_n)} \geq \dfrac{a(\Bar{\psi}_n,\Bar{\psi}_n)}{1+v}=\dfrac{a}{1+v} \left[1- \sum_I |(\phi_I,\psi_n)|^2\right] \\
L000733	& = \dfrac{a}{1+v} \left[1-v^2 \sum_I |(\phi_I,w^1\psi_n)|^2\right] \geq \dfrac{a}{1+v} \left[1-v^2 ||w^1 \psi_n||^2\right] \geq \dfrac{a}{1+v} \left[1-v^2 \right] =a(1-v) \, . \\
L000734	\end{split}
L000735	\end{equation}
L000736	
L000737	\vspace{-0.2cm}
L000738	\subsection{Additional remarks}
L000739	\vspace{-0.2cm}
L000740	
L000741	Our proof above can be safely generalized to a broader class of kinetic theories. For example, one can relax the assumption that the particle number is conserved. In that case, the proof is essentially identical: we just need to remove $\delta J^\mu$ from the conserved currents, and $1$ from the kernel of $I$. We can also allow for degenerate statistics, in which case one should replace $f_{\text{eq}} d^3 p$ with $g_s f_{\text{eq}}(1\pm f_{\text{eq}}) d^3p$ in the inner products, where $g_s$ is the spin degeneracy.
L000742	
L000743	\newpage
L000744	\section*{Boosted non-hydrodynamic spectrum of randomly-generated models}
L000745	
L000746	\vspace{-0.2cm}
L000747	\subsubsection{Without rotational symmetry in the rest frame}
L000748	\vspace{-0.2cm}
L000749	
L000750	We generate random models according to the following procedure. Since one may always choose a basis in $\Psi$--space such that $\E^0\,{=}\,1$ and $\sigma\,{=}\,\mathrm{diag}(\sigma_n)$, we impose these conditions from the outset. The non-zero diagonal entries $\sigma_n$ are then sampled as independent random variables uniformly distributed between $0$ and $1$ (in natural units).
L000751	To construct $\E^1$, we generate a random matrix $\mathbb{M}$ with entries uniformly distributed in the interval $[-1,1]$, and set $\E^1 \,{=}\, w\,(\mathbb{M}{+}\mathbb{M}^T)/\lVert \mathbb{M}{+}\mathbb{M}^T\rVert$. This normalization ensures that $\lVert \E^1\rVert = w$.
